## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical governance, privacy compliance, and performance in multi-tenant LLM ops, including policy versioning, KMS integration, and audit-ability under latency pressure.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant redaction policies (regex/ML)
- Tamper-evident, region-local audit logs
- KMS-backed policy keys and versioning
- Drift checks and alerting
- Latency considerations: streaming redaction

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code sketch: redaction path
function redactPromptsAndOutputs(prompt, policy) {
  const redactedIn = applyRedaction(prompt, policy.inputRules);
  const redactedOut = applyRedaction(await llm(redactedIn), policy.outputRules);
  return { redactedIn, redactedOut };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate and test latency under burst traffic?
- How would you verify tamper-evident integrity in audit logs?

## Why This Is Asked

Assess ability to implement low-latency, per-tenant policy gating with live hot-swaps, immutability guarantees, and rollback strategies in a production LLM-ops gateway.

## Key Concepts

- Per-brand policy isolation and versioning
- Immutable policy versions with blue/green rollout
- Fast policy evaluation (sub-20 ms) and robust auditing
- WASM/Rust prototype for safe, sandboxed evaluation

## Code Example

```rust
// Minimal WASM-friendly policy evaluator API
pub fn eval(input: &str, policy_json: &str) -> bool {
    // naive policy: blocklist example
    if policy_json.contains("blocked") {
        return !input.contains("blocked");
    }
    true
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you monitor policy drift and trigger automatic rollbacks?
- What metrics would you collect to ensure low false negatives with high safety?
